/**

 @page page_exahype_multicore Tasking (multicore parallelisation)
 
  
 ExaHyPE starts from @ref page_exahype_domain-decomposition "plain domain decomposition".
 Its domain decomposition splits up the domain over multiple MPI ranks, but it also
 can make one rank hold multiple subpartitions, and these subpartitions are then 
 processed by different threads.
 On top of this domain decomposition, you can add tasking which only works per rank.
 That is, if you give each rank only one subdomain and if you add tasking on top, 
 then you can genuine task parallelsim.
 Typically, this is a poor idea, i.e. you are better off with a mixture of domain 
 decomposition and tasking to exploit many threads.
 Further to that, only some ExaHyPE solvers (typically those with an Enclave in
 their name) support tasking and therefore benefit from the discussion below.
 

 If you have a solver with tasking and want to activate this tasking, you have 
 to do the following steps: 

 1. @ref page_installation_home "Configure" Peano with multithreading support.
 2. Pick a proper @ref page_exahype_domain-decomposition "load balancing scheme" no matter if you want subpartitions per 
    rank or not.
 3. Peak a solver that supports tasking.
 4. Tweak the task orchestration as discussed below.
 5. Run some @ref page_exahype_runtime_analysis "performance analysis".
 6. Study the @ref page_exahype_performance_optimisation "optimisation recommendations".
 
 
 ## Using ExaHyPE 2 with tasks 

 All instructions how to enable tasking support is collected in a @ref tutorials_exahype2_hpc_tasking "multicore/tasking tutorial".
 While the tutorial provides a deep-dive, the key ingredients can be summarised by two simple headlines:
 
 ### Pick a solver that supports tasking

 Most ExaHyPE solvers supporting tasking have something alike Enclave in their solver name.
 These solvers spawn tasks.
 In principle, no further work should be required for these solvers and they behave exactly the same way as their non-tasking counterparts.
 
 ### Tweak the orchestration strategy
 
 The orchestration strategy determines how the task fusion is realised under the hood.
 In which way this yields a high throughput is subject of discussion on the 
 @ref documentation_tarch_task_fusion_strategies "page on task fusion".
 
 Switching the strategy is possible in ExaHyPE has to happen within the C++ code.
 However, ExaHyPE provides a routine through exahype2.Project.set_multicore_orchestration()
 into which we can inject a C++ call instantiating a different orchestration scheme:
 
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 project.set_multicore_orchestration( """new ::tarch::multicore::orchestration::Hardcoded(
  32,256,
  tarch::multicore::Task::Host,
  ::tarch::multicore::orchestration::Hardcoded::NoNestedParallelism,
  ::tarch::multicore::orchestration::FusionImplementation::EagerConsumerTasks
)""" )
 project.add_mainfile_include("tarch/multicore/orchestration/Hardcoded.h")
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

 While the Python routine is trivial - it simply accepts a string which 
 instantiates an orchestration objecton the heap - you have to study the 
 orchestration objects provided, or you have to write your own one. All
 orchestration classes have to inherit from tarch::multicore::orchestration::Strategy. 
  
 If you do nothing like this, then the orchestration will be determined by
 whatever tarch::multicore::orchestration::createDefaultStrategy() returns.
 Plesae note that it is your responsibility to add the corresponding includes
 here as well, so the solver repository knows about the orchestration object
 that you want to use.
 
 ### OpenMP
 
 Peano's tasking requires OpenMP 6 upwards. 
 If you don't have that and want to use task fusion nevertheless, you have to employ timed execution.
 The rationale are presented at @ref documentation_tarch_task_fusion_strategies.
 ExaHyPE by default makes all task fusion "deteriorate" to timed fusion by default if you enable OpenMP but haven't got OpenMP6 yet.
 
  
 ## How the tasking works

 The solvers in ExaHyPE that support tasking work directly with the 
 abstractions of the multithreading as it is provided by the 
 @ref page_tarch_multicore "technical architecture". All the documentation
 how tasking works in Peano can be found there.
   

 ## Adding tasks to your ExaHyPE solver (programmer view)

 */
 
 